<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="writing-linear-equations">
    <title>Writing Equations of Lines</title>

    <p>
        A linear function has a constant rate of change, called the
        <term>slope</term>. Once we know the slope and some information
        about the line, we can write an equation that models the
        relationship between the input and output variables.
    </p>

    <subsection xml:id="slope-intercept-form">
        <title>Slope-Intercept Form</title>

        <p>
            If we know the slope of a line and its vertical intercept,
            we can write the equation in <term>slope-intercept form</term>.
        </p>

        <me>
                y=mx+b
        </me>

        <ul>
            <li><m>m</m> is the slope (rate of change).</li>
            <li><m>b</m> is the <m>y</m>-intercept (the output when <m>x=0</m>).</li>
        </ul>

        <example xml:id="lake-b-example">
            <title>Lake Depth</title>

            <p>
                A scientist measures the depth of a lake. On day 7, the
                lake is 16.3 meters deep, and on day 28, the lake is
                20.5 meters deep.
            </p>

            <p>
                First, find the slope:
            </p>

            <md>
                <mrow>
                    <m>m=\frac{20.5-16.3}{28-7}</m>
                    <m>=\frac{4.2}{21}</m>
                    <m>=0.2</m>
                </mrow>
            </md>

            <p>
                The lesson notes give the model
            </p>

            <md>
                <m>B(x)=0.2x+14.9</m>
            </md>

            <p>
                This equation is in slope-intercept form. The slope
                tells us that the lake depth increases by
                0.2 meters per day, and the intercept tells us that
                the lake was 14.9 meters deep on day 0.
            </p>
        </example>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="point-slope-form">
        <title>Point-Slope Form</title>

        <p>
            Sometimes we know the slope of a line and one point on the
            line. In this case, it is often easiest to use
            <term>point-slope form</term>.
        </p>

        <md>
            <mrow>
                <m>y-y_1=m(x-x_1)</m>
            </mrow>
        </md>

        <p>
            Here, <m>(x_1,y_1)</m> is any point on the line and
            <m>m</m> is the slope.
        </p>

        <example xml:id="cereal-example">
            <title>Amount of Cereal Remaining</title>

            <p>
                Dr. Infinity has a new box of cereal. After one day,
                there are 17.5 ounces remaining. After four days,
                there are 10 ounces remaining.
            </p>

            <p>
                The two points are <m>(1,17.5)</m> and
                <m>(4,10)</m>.
            </p>

            <p>
                First, find the slope:
            </p>

            <md>
                <mrow>
                    <m>m=\frac{10-17.5}{4-1}</m>
                    <m>=\frac{-7.5}{3}</m>
                    <m>=-2.5</m>
                </mrow>
            </md>

            <p>
                Using point-slope form with the point
                <m>(1,17.5)</m>:
            </p>

            <md>
                <m>A(x)-17.5=-2.5(x-1)</m>
            </md>

            <p>
                This equation is correct and fully describes the line.
            </p>

            <p>
                If we distribute and simplify, we obtain slope-intercept form:
            </p>

            <md>
                <mrow>
                    <m>A(x)-17.5=-2.5x+2.5</m>
                </mrow>
            </md>

            <md>
                <mrow>
                    <m>A(x)=-2.5x+20</m>
                </mrow>
            </md>

            <p>
                This tells us that the cereal decreases by
                2.5 ounces each day and that the box originally
                contained 20 ounces of cereal.
            </p>
        </example>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="choosing-a-form">
        <title>Choosing a Form</title>

        <p>
            Both slope-intercept form and point-slope form describe the
            same line. Choose the form that matches the information you
            are given:
        </p>

        <ul>
            <li>
                Use <m>y=mx+b</m> when you know the slope and the
                <m>y</m>-intercept.
            </li>
            <li>
                Use <m>y-y_1=m(x-x_1)</m> when you know the slope and
                a point on the line.
            </li>
            <li>
                If given two points, first find the slope, then use
                point-slope form or convert to slope-intercept form.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </subsection>


